WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.535
The Cyber Kill Chain is a defensive model for thinking about how cyber intrusions progress.

00:00:05.535 --> 00:00:09.884
Lockheed Martin developed the framework as part of an Intelligence Driven Defense

00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:12.420
approach for identifying and preventing intrusion activity.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.768
The model describes a sequence of things an adversary would need to

00:00:16.768 --> 00:00:18.580
accomplish to reach an objective:

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.479
reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control,

00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:22.928
and actions on objectives.

00:00:22.928 --> 00:00:26.189
For defenders, the value is not the diagram itself.

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:29.451
The value is the set of questions it creates.

00:00:29.451 --> 00:00:31.987
What stage does the evidence actually support?

00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:34.886
Where could the activity have been detected earlier?

00:00:34.886 --> 00:00:38.872
Which control might deny, disrupt, degrade, deceive, or contain the behavior?

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:42.133
What telemetry would confirm or challenge the current assessment?

00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:45.032
The Cyber Kill Chain gives analysts, responders, engineers,

00:00:45.032 --> 00:00:49.380
and leaders a shared vocabulary for discussing intrusion progress and defensive opportunities.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.728
It should be used with evidence, threat intelligence, business context, and judgment.

00:00:53.728 --> 00:00:58.801
Not every incident follows a clean sequence, and some activity may be compressed, repeated,

00:00:58.801 --> 00:01:01.338
automated, or invisible to the available logs.

00:01:01.338 --> 00:01:05.686
Used well, the model helps teams organize evidence, improve detections, prioritize controls,

00:01:05.686 --> 00:01:09.309
and communicate risk without turning analysis into a rigid checklist.

00:01:09.309 --> 00:01:13.658
A useful way to introduce the model to a team is to

00:01:13.658 --> 00:01:18.006
treat each stage as a chance to ask what the defender can

00:01:18.006 --> 00:01:19.093
see and influence.

00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:21.267
Earlier disruption usually reduces later impact,

00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:25.253
but late-stage evidence can still be critical for recovery, legal review,

00:01:25.253 --> 00:01:26.340
and control improvement.
